When `git-annex p2phttp` is serving many git repositories at the same time,
it should avoid running a large number of helper processes like
`git cat-file` and `git hash-object`. To avoid running out of process
limits.

Currently for N repositories, there are N cat-files,
but `(min jobs cpus) * N` hash-objects. (And the same for check-attr and
check-ignore, although I don't think p2phttp would usually run those.)

The point of that parallelization is that hashing is a cpu bound operation
and so can parallelize. But that was implemented assuming git-annex is
running in 1 repository, while with p2phttp it can be running in multiple
repositories. (With a large number of local git remotes it might also be
possible for the same kind of problem to happen.)

Even the 1 cat-file per repository can be too many, if there are thousands
of repositories, eventually there will be thousands of cat-files, most of
them long idle, and it will eventually run out of process limits.

Seems to me that this needs a pool that is in some way shared amoung all the
repositories, and is capped out at some reasonable maximum number of
processes. So when a new cat-file or hash-object is needed and the pool is
full, it can find a currently unused process running in some other 
repository and free it.

The reasonable maximum number is more than the number of cpus, because
stopping and later restarting one of these processes is not a free
operation. Some fraction of the maximum number of processes might be a good
value. --[[Joey]]

[[!tag projects/ICE4]]
